# The index is LF. WG15 (Windows) clones set core.autocrlf=true, which without
# a catch-all checks the whole tree out CRLF. That is not cosmetic: oxfmt and
# ESLint behave differently on CRLF than on the LF tree CI checks out.
# Concretely, on a CRLF worktree oxfmt emits one `Delete ␍` per line and never
# reaches its other checks, so import-order and line-width violations are
# INVISIBLE locally while CI fails on them; comment-length also counts the
# trailing `\r` against maxLength and reports fixes it cannot make. `git diff`
# then shows nothing (the CR is normalised away on the way into the object
# store) while `git status` shows the whole tree modified, so the churn is easy
# to commit by accident.
#
# Pin every text file to LF. Byte-exact fixtures opt out in their own
# directory's .gitattributes (e.g. packages/luau-ast/test/fixtures/cst uses
# `*.luau -text` for the CRLF fidelity fixtures), which takes precedence over
# this catch-all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# pnpm pack (ERR_PNPM_BIN_CRLF) rejects a CRLF shebang: with core.autocrlf=true
# bin/* used to check out CRLF and tsdown build failed during game-repo setup.
bin/** text eol=lf

# `luau/*.luau` is not compiled — it is spliced verbatim into generated test
# scripts by string replacement (`src/backends/result-relay.ts`). A CRLF
# checkout makes every spliced line end `\r\n`, so the injected script stops
# matching the `\n`-joined text its tests assert on, and a generated Luau
# program can carry stray carriage returns into Roblox.
luau/** text eol=lf
